WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.880
This week, NASA captured a new impact on the Moon,

00:02.880 --> 00:06.320
completed another spacewalk, and tested the future of high-speed flight.

00:06.320 --> 00:09.280
Here's what's new in your NASA Minute.

00:09.280 --> 00:12.960
NASA has captured the aftermath of a rocket-stage impact on the Moon.

00:12.960 --> 00:16.720
New images from the lunar reconnaissance orbiter show the 60-foot crater

00:16.720 --> 00:19.680
that was formed when a SpaceX Falcon 9 upper-stage

00:19.680 --> 00:22.080
struck the lunar surface earlier this month,

00:22.080 --> 00:26.800
giving scientists a closer look at how the Moon changed after impact.

00:26.880 --> 00:30.800
Another spacewalk is in the books aboard the International Space Station.

00:30.800 --> 00:34.880
On Tuesday, NASA astronaut Neil Mennon and ESA astronaut Sophie Adano

00:34.880 --> 00:38.000
spent over six hours outside the orbiting laboratory,

00:38.000 --> 00:41.840
removing a failed space-to-ground antenna and preparing to install its replacement.

00:42.800 --> 00:46.480
NASA is putting the future of aircraft wings to the test.

00:46.480 --> 00:50.400
At Langley Research Center, researchers used pressure-sensitive paint,

00:50.400 --> 00:55.520
high-speed cameras, and a wind tunnel to study how flexible wings perform in flutter conditions,

00:55.600 --> 00:59.040
helping close a key design gap for future high-speed aircraft.

00:59.920 --> 01:03.840
NASA's work to achieve the near impossible is continuing on every frontier.

01:03.840 --> 01:11.200
We'll see you next time in the NASA Minute.

